#{[17592186564271 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564271 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564271 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c4543906-53e0-543b-8b49-5cef8732311e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564401 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564401 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564401 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186564168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564401 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5ff271c5-90b5-5fb1-aa9a-8de6b8aed244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564488 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564488 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564488 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=x\\,i-z\\,j+y\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=2\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "598960e7-33b5-506e-acd3-0cfad9c377b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564488 #edb/ref 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i-z\\,j+y\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\pi \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\pi [t]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
